<p>You've done the research. You've watched the videos. You've picked the breed. You're ready.</p>

<p>But there's one number nobody gives you before you bring a dog home — the real first year total. Not the estimated annual cost. The actual, itemized, nothing-left-out number for year one.</p>

<p>Because year one is different. It's not just food and vet visits. It's the year you buy everything from scratch, pay for surgery you didn't plan for, and discover three expenses you never knew existed.</p>

<h2>Why the First Year Costs More Than Any Other Year</h2>

<p>Most pet cost estimates show you the ongoing annual cost — what you'll spend in year three or year five when things have settled down. That number looks manageable. Year one is a different story. You're paying for:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>The purchase price</strong> — $500 to $8,000+ depending on breed</li>
<li><strong>Everything to set up a home for a dog</strong> — crate, bed, bowls, leash, collar, harness, baby gates</li>
<li><strong>Spay or neuter surgery</strong> — a one-time cost of $300-$600</li>
<li><strong>First-year vaccines</strong> — puppies need a series of shots in their first months</li>
<li><strong>Training</strong> — strongly recommended, especially for high-energy breeds</li>
<li><strong>All the mistakes</strong> — chewed furniture, emergency vet visits, toys that last three days</li>
</ul>

