<h2>The Costs Most First-Time Owners Miss</h2>

<h3>1. The puppy tax on everything</h3>
<p>Puppies destroy things. Budget $200-$500 for items you will replace in the first year — shoes left out, furniture legs chewed, leashes eaten through. This is not a maybe. It is a when.</p>

<h3>2. Training is not optional</h3>
<p>An untrained dog costs more than a trained one — in destroyed property, in vet visits from stress-related behaviors, and in the eventual professional training you will need anyway. Budget $250-$800 for professional training in year one.</p>

<h3>3. The emergency fund gap</h3>
<p>The average first-time dog owner has no emergency vet fund. The average emergency vet visit costs $800-$1,500. Before you bring a dog home, put $2,000 in a separate savings account and don't touch it unless a vet bill arrives.</p>

<h3>4. Pet insurance timing</h3>
<p>Pet insurance must be purchased before a condition is diagnosed. The moment your vet notes a possible hip issue, that condition becomes pre-existing and uncoverable. Enroll on day one — not after the first vet visit.</p>

<h3>5. Boarding and sitting costs</h3>
<p>If you travel even once in year one, you will pay for boarding or pet sitting. Budget $300-$700 for this even if you don't think you will need it. Life happens.</p>

<h2>The Shopping List — What to Buy Before Day One</h2>

<ul>
<li>Crate (appropriately sized) — $40-$150</li>
<li>Dog bed — $30-$100</li>
<li>Food and water bowls — $15-$40</li>
<li>Collar, leash, harness — $40-$80</li>
<li>ID tags — $10-$20</li>
<li>Enzymatic cleaner (for accidents) — $15</li>
<li>Baby gate — $30-$80</li>
<li>Dog food (ask breeder what they have been eating) — $30-$80</li>
<li>Puppy toys (2-3 varieties) — $30-$60</li>
<li>Nail clippers — $15-$30</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Total pre-arrival spend: $230-$645</strong></p>

<h2>The Number That Surprises People Most</h2>

<p>When first-time dog owners are asked how much they think a dog costs in year one, the average answer is $1,200. The real answer is $2,500-$5,000 before purchase price — and $3,500-$9,000 when you include what you paid for the dog.</p>

<p>That gap between expected and actual is why this article exists. Go in with accurate numbers and you will be a better, less stressed, more financially prepared dog owner.</p>

<blockquote>Your dog deserves an owner who planned ahead.</blockquote>

